1. STC89C52 Microcontroller
STC89C52 is an 8-bit microcontroller based on the 8051 architecture. It contains a CPU, memory, registers, GPIO ports, timers and interrupts. These components work together to control electronic devices.
The main benefit of a microcontroller is that several required elements are integrated into one device instead of being added separately.
2. CPU Architecture
The CPU is the main part of the microcontroller. It receives instructions, understands them, performs the required operation and produces the result.
Main CPU parts:• ALU (Arithmetic Logic Unit)• Control Unit• Registers• Program Counter (PC)• Stack Pointer (SP)• Status / Flag registers
Basic operation: Fetch → Decode → Execute
3. Registers
Registers are small storage locations inside the CPU used for temporarily storing data and addresses during program execution.
Main registers:• Accumulator / A register• B register• R0 to R7 registers• Program Counter• Stack Pointer• PSW / Program Status Word
4. A Register / Accumulator
The A register is an 8-bit register also called the Accumulator (ACC). It is mainly used for arithmetic, logical and data-transfer operations.
Example:MOV A, #25H
This loads 25H into the A register.
5. B Register and R0–R7
The B register is an 8-bit register mainly used with A for multiplication and division.
Examples:MUL ABDIV AB
R0 to R7 are general-purpose 8-bit registers used for temporarily storing data, counting and other small operations. The 8051 architecture has multiple register banks selected through PSW.
6. Program Counter and Stack Pointer
The Program Counter (PC) is a 16-bit register that stores the address of the next instruction to be fetched from program memory. Jump and branch instructions can change the PC.
The Stack Pointer (SP) is an 8-bit register used to point to the top of the stack. The stack follows LIFO (Last In, First Out). After reset, SP is 07H. During PUSH, SP is increased first and the data is stored.
7. PSW / Program Status Word
PSW contains status information and control bits related to CPU operations.
Important bits:• CY – Carry flag• AC – Auxiliary Carry• F0 – User flag• RS1 and RS0 – Register bank selection• OV – Overflow flag• P – Parity flag
CY indicates a carry from arithmetic operations. AC indicates carry from the lower four bits. OV indicates signed arithmetic overflow. P indicates the parity of the value in A.
8. Memory and Stack
Memory is used for storing program and data.
Program Memory stores the instructions that the CPU executes. The CPU fetches instructions using the Program Counter.
Data Memory stores data required during execution. Internal RAM contains register banks, a bit-addressable area and general-purpose RAM.
The stack is a special memory area for temporary storage. PUSH places data into the stack and POP removes it. The stack is also used during CALL, RET and interrupt operations.
9. Instruction Categories and Execution
Instruction categories include:
• Data Transfer – MOV, PUSH, POP• Arithmetic – ADD, ADDC, SUBB, INC, DEC, MUL, DIV• Logical – ANL, ORL, XRL, CLR, CPL• Branch / Control – SJMP, LJMP, AJMP, JZ, JNZ, DJNZ• Bit Operations – SETB, CLR, CPL
Basic instruction execution:Fetch → Decode → Execute → Write Back
Example:MOV A, #25HBefore execution: A = 00HAfter execution: A = 25H
10. GPIO
GPIO means General Purpose Input/Output. GPIO pins communicate with external devices. A GPIO pin can work as an input to receive information or an output to send a signal.
The four main I/O ports are:• Port 0 – P0.0 to P0.7• Port 1 – P1.0 to P1.7• Port 2 – P2.0 to P2.7• Port 3 – P3.0 to P3.7
Each port has 8 pins. A button can be connected as an input and an LED can be controlled as an output.
11. Timer / Counter
A timer counts clock events inside the microcontroller, while a counter counts external events. Timers are useful for delays, timing operations and periodic tasks.
The architecture has Timer 0 and Timer 1. Important timer registers include:• TMOD• TCON• TH0 / TL0• TH1 / TL1
Timer overflow can be used to generate an interrupt.
12. Interrupts
An interrupt allows the CPU to temporarily stop its current program and respond to an important event. The Interrupt Service Routine (ISR) handles the event.
Flow:Main Program → Interrupt Occurs → Identify Source → Execute ISR → Return → Continue Main Program
Interrupt sources include:• External Interrupt 0• Timer 0• External Interrupt 1• Timer 1• Serial communication
Interrupts can be enabled, disabled and prioritized using registers such as IE and IP. RETI returns execution to the main program.
13. Overall Working
The STC89C52 CPU gets instructions from program memory using the Program Counter. The instruction is fetched, decoded and executed. Registers such as A, B and R0–R7 store and process data. PSW stores status and flag information, while SP manages the stack.
GPIO ports communicate with external devices, timers perform timing and counting operations, and interrupts allow the CPU to respond to important events. All these parts work together to make the STC89C52 perform different tasks.
14. Conclusion
This study covers the main CPU and architecture concepts of the STC89C52 microcontroller, including CPU operation, registers, PSW, memory, stack, instructions, GPIO, timers and interrupts. Understanding how these components work together provides a foundation for studying and simulating an 8051-compatible microcontroller system.
